in this lesson we're going to talk about  rationalizing denominators so these  problems oftentimes occur on the act see  if you can do these two examples we'll  go through them together talk about how  to rationalize denominators so the first  one it says which is equivalent to 3  divided by the square root of 2 minus 1.  we've got some multiple choice options  here and example 2 says which is  equivalent to 4 divided by square root  of 3 minus i where i equals square root  of negative 1 and again we've got some  multiple choice options so go ahead and  pause the video see if you can do these  on your own and we'll go through them  together  okay so the first one we don't want this  radical in the denominator that's  considered improper so how do we get rid  of that radical well when it's a  binomial like this what you want to do  is you want to multiply by the conjugate  now what's the conjugate well the  conjugate is going to be the same two  terms that we have here except for we're  going to change that sign in between  these two terms to the opposite so you  can see here i have square root of 2  minus 1 we're going to multiply by  square root of 2 plus 1  but whatever you do to the denominator  you want to do to the numerator because  anything divided by itself is 1 and then  1 times this fraction will  have the same value it'll just be in a  different format  so what we're going to do here is we're  going to think of this as a group okay  like so  and we're going to distribute the 3 to  both of these terms so that gives us 3  square root of 2  plus 3 okay and then here we're going to  foil or do the distributive property  twice depending how you think about it  so square root of 2 times square root of  2  is going to give us 2 because square  root of 2 times square root of 2 is  square root of 4 square root of 4 is 2  and then square root of 2 times 1 gives  us positive square root of 2 and then  the negative 1 when we distribute gives  us negative square root of 2 and  negative 1 times positive 1 gives us  negative 1. okay one thing that you'll  notice here is that the  inside terms the inner and the outer are  going to cancel one another out so  square root of 2 and negative square  root of 2 they cancel  2 minus 1 of course is just 1. and so  we've got 3 square root of 2 plus 3 all  divided by 1. anything divided by 1 is  itself  so it looks like the best answer here is  going to be letter c  3 square root of 2 plus 3.  okay let's go on to example number 2.  it says which is equivalent to 4 divided  by the square root of 3 minus i where i  equals the square root of negative 1.  okay a couple things going on here we've  got a radical in the denominator as well  as an i the imaginary i right we both we  don't want square roots in the  denominator we also don't want i's in  the denominator so what we're going to  do is we're going to multiply by the  conjugate just like we did here except  for this at a time it's called the  complex conjugate because this is a  complex number it's part real part  imaginary but all we really need to know  is  change that sign in between the two  terms to the opposite and so we're going  to do the same thing to the numerator  and the denominator and if we do this  let's see what we get we get 4 times  square root of 3  which is 4 square root of 3 4 times i  which is 4i  and then in the denominator we get  square root of 3 times square root of 3  which is 3 because that's square root of  9 right square minus 3. square root of 3  times i just gives us square root of 3  i okay square root of 3i  negative i times square root of 3 gives  us negative square root of 3i  and then negative i times positive i  gives us negative i squared  okay now we talked about this in a  another lesson but i squared is actually  equal to negative 1  and times this negative would make this  a positive 1 plus 3 is 4.  the square root of 3i and the negative  square root of 3i they cancel one  another out and so we're left with 4  square root of 3 plus 4i all over 4. now  what you can do here is you can split  this up into two groups  like that so split it up into two  fractions and then you can divide you  know these fours so that will just give  you square root of three and then these  fours which will just give you i so the  best answer here is going to be square  root of three plus i and that's going to  be  letter b so this is how you rationalize  radicals and  eliminate the radical and denominator or  the i in the denominator if you just  have a monomial i'll just mention real  quick here like one term like say it's  square root of two you don't have to  multiply by the conjugate you can just  multiply by square root of 2 over square  root of 2 because square root of 2 times  square root of 2 is 2. there you go  you've eliminated the square root of the  denominator and then you have 4 square  root of 2 in the numerator and then the  4 and the 2 those are both integers you  can reduce those and just get 2 square  root of 2. but if it's a binomial like  these you want to multiply by the  conjugate i'll see in the next lesson
 

